  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nToday, shower and thunderstorm activity is anticipated during \nthe afternoon and evening hours under a broad and amplified \ntrough with the parent low pressure system traversing across the\nGreat Lakes. This feature will have activity ahead of its cold\nfrontal boundary with some of the storms having the potential \nto become strong to severe. Modest instability and shear will \nsupport this and SPC has us in mainly a slight threat for \nsevere thunderstorms across the entire area. \n\nThe main threats will be damaging wind although hail cannot be \nruled out along with isolated flash flooding possible. PWATs \nare not nearly as impressive as yesterday, however they do \napproach the 1.3 to 1.5 mark along with very high DCAPE around \n1000 J/kg and low level dry air to allow for downburst and heavy\ndownpours which would promote possible flooding issues if \nrepeatedly hit by heavy thunderstorm activity. One hour flash \nflood guidance is low across the board so any of these storms \nthat produce more than guidance is likely to cause an issue if \nrepeated hit or train over the same area, especially across low\nlying and flood prone areas. \n\nModels have come together on frontal passage taking place\ntonight with thunderstorm activity possible into the overnight\nhowever the severe threat will have diminished by then. The \nHi-res and CAM models have scattered activity during the \nafternoon and evening hours. The HRRR is most aggressive as \nwell as the other CAMs that would support the slight risk for \nsevere thunderstorms or at least scattered thunderstorms that \nhave the potential to be strong to severe. \n\nWPC had us in a marginal threat for excessive rainfall until\nthis morning although have taken us out of the risk on the\nlatest update. Areas who are most susceptible to flooding issues\nare the aforementioned low lying and flood prone areas as well \nas areas who have had significant flooding lately and who's \nflash flood guidance is lower than the average across the area \nwhich is below about 1.5 inches per hour. The aspect we have \ngoing for us is that steering flow will be supported by decent \nupper level dynamics and will allow for storms to be moving \nrelatively at a good pace. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAs cold frontal passage takes place tonight northwesterly flow \nunder a broad upper level trough will induce dry air into the \narea lowering dewpoints into the 60s during the daytime and 50s\nat nighttime over the next several days. Moisture starved and \nunder surface high pressure building in we can expect a dry \nspell basically through midweek although some lingering showers \nin the northeast mountains will be possible from Sunday into \nMonday morning, but the chances will be low.\n\nThereafter, a low pressure system is expected to traverse the \nregion across the Great Lakes and promote unsettled weather on \nThursday. After that, diurnally driven activity will be \npossible going into the weekend under numerous short waves \nforcast to traverse the area, therefore the dry spell will come\nto an end by late Wednesday.",
